CAT = "cmaLab"; SEG = "LAB"
Q = [
("A urine reagent strip is positive for nitrite. What does this most often suggest?",
 "Bacteria in the urine", ["Protein loss from the kidneys", "High blood glucose", "A recent heavy meal"],
 "Many urinary bacteria convert nitrate to nitrite, so a positive nitrite points to a urinary tract infection. Leukocyte esterase, which detects white cells, often appears with it."),
("A patient's urine dipstick shows ketones. What does this indicate?",
 "Fat is being burned for energy", ["The kidneys are filtering protein", "The urine sample is too dilute", "There is blood in the bladder"],
 "Ketones form when fat is broken down because glucose is unavailable, as in fasting, very low-carbohydrate diets or uncontrolled diabetes."),
("Casts seen in a urine sediment under the microscope are formed in which part of the urinary system?",
 "Kidney tubules", ["The bladder wall", "The urethra", "The ureters"],
 "Casts are cylinders molded inside the kidney tubules, so they point to kidney disease rather than a bladder problem."),
("What does a hematocrit measure?",
 "Percent of blood that is red cells", ["Grams of hemoglobin per deciliter", "Number of white cells per drop", "Time it takes blood to clot"],
 "Hematocrit is the share of whole blood volume made up of packed red blood cells. Hemoglobin is reported separately in grams per deciliter."),
("A patient's erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) is high. What does this most generally indicate?",
 "Inflammation in the body", ["A specific bacterial infection", "Low blood glucose levels", "A clotting factor deficiency"],
 "ESR is a nonspecific marker: red cells settle faster when inflammatory proteins are raised. It does not identify the cause or site."),
("A hemoglobin A1C result reflects average blood glucose over about how long?",
 "The past 2 to 3 months", ["The past 24 hours", "The past week", "The past 12 months or so"],
 "Glucose binds to hemoglobin for the life of the red cell (about 120 days), so A1C reflects roughly the last 2 to 3 months. No fasting is needed."),
("A rapid mononucleosis test is ordered for a teenager with fatigue and swollen glands. What does it detect?",
 "Heterophile antibodies", ["Group A strep antigen", "Influenza A protein", "Human chorionic gonadotropin"],
 "Infectious mononucleosis (Epstein-Barr virus) produces heterophile antibodies that the rapid slide or cassette test detects."),
("A breath or stool test for Helicobacter pylori is ordered. Which condition is this bacterium linked to?",
 "Peptic ulcers", ["Kidney stones", "Strep throat", "Urinary infections"],
 "H. pylori infects the stomach lining and causes most peptic ulcers. Treatment combines antibiotics with acid reducers."),
("A skin scraping is mixed with potassium hydroxide (KOH) on a slide. What is the provider looking for?",
 "Fungal hyphae or yeast", ["Red blood cell casts", "Pinworm eggs and larvae", "Gram-positive cocci"],
 "KOH dissolves skin cells and keratin but leaves fungal elements, so hyphae and yeast stand out. A saline wet mount is used for organisms such as trichomonads."),
("A medical assistant has one tube of blood to spin. How is the centrifuge balanced?",
 "Equal tube of water opposite it", ["Place the tube in any slot", "Run it at half speed for safety", "Tape the tube to the rotor"],
 "An unbalanced centrifuge vibrates, can break tubes and damage the machine. A tube of equal volume (water) goes in the slot directly opposite."),
("The provider orders a chemistry panel on serum. What is done with the blood?",
 "Let it clot, then centrifuge", ["Mix with EDTA, then spin", "Keep it on ice unspun", "Spin it right away with heparin"],
 "Serum is the liquid left after blood clots, so it is drawn in a tube without anticoagulant and allowed to clot before spinning. Plasma comes from anticoagulated blood."),
("On a standard light microscope, which objective requires a drop of immersion oil?",
 "100x objective", ["4x objective", "10x objective", "40x objective"],
 "The 100x oil immersion lens needs oil between the lens and slide to keep light from scattering. Using oil on the 40x lens damages it."),
("Why is a chain-of-custody form used for a workplace drug screen?",
 "To document everyone who handled it", ["To list each drug being screened for", "To record the patient's insurance", "To show the provider's diagnosis"],
 "Chain of custody records every person who handled the specimen, with times and signatures, so the result can stand up legally."),
("A patient's distance vision is 20/40 in the right eye. What does this mean?",
 "Sees at 20 ft what most see at 40 ft", ["Sees at 40 ft what most see at 20 ft", "Has 40 percent of normal vision", "Read 20 of 40 letters correctly"],
 "The top number is the testing distance and the bottom is the distance at which a person with normal vision could read the same line. 20/40 is worse than 20/20."),
("Which test is used in the office to screen for color vision deficiency?",
 "Ishihara color plates", ["Snellen letter chart at 20 ft", "Jaeger card", "Tonometry"],
 "Ishihara plates hide numbers in dots of color that people with red-green deficiency cannot see. Jaeger cards test near vision; tonometry measures eye pressure."),
("What does tympanometry measure?",
 "How well the eardrum moves", ["Hearing at each pitch", "Amount of earwax in the canal", "Balance while standing"],
 "Tympanometry changes air pressure in the canal and records eardrum movement, which shows fluid behind the eardrum or a perforation. Pure-tone audiometry tests hearing at each pitch."),
("A provider orders a RAST test for a patient with suspected food allergies. What kind of test is it?",
 "Blood test for allergen IgE", ["Scratches on the forearm", "Patches taped to the back", "Injection just under the skin"],
 "The radioallergosorbent test measures IgE antibodies to specific allergens in a blood sample, so it does not risk a reaction on the skin. Scratch, intradermal and patch tests are skin tests."),
("During spirometry, what does the FEV1 value measure?",
 "Air forced out in 1 second", ["Total air the lungs can hold", "Air left after a full breath out", "Breaths taken in one minute"],
 "FEV1 is the volume exhaled in the first second of a forced breath. It is compared with the total forced vital capacity to look for airway obstruction."),
("A patient is scheduled for an MRI. Which question is most important to ask beforehand?",
 "Any pacemaker or metal implants?", ["Any recent dental cleaning?", "Any allergy to latex gloves?", "Any family history of skin cancer?"],
 "MRI uses a powerful magnet, so pacemakers, some implants and metal fragments can be dangerous. The imaging center screens every patient for metal."),
("A patient's thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is high. Which condition does this usually suggest?",
 "Hypothyroidism", ["Hyperthyroidism", "Type 1 diabetes", "Adrenal insufficiency"],
 "When the thyroid makes too little hormone, the pituitary raises TSH to push it harder. A low TSH usually goes with an overactive thyroid."),
]
